WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.423
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.370
YUTTY KAWAHARA: Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.260
This session is to
explain how Dragon Quest

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.750
Walk, a game currently
very popular in Japan,

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.630
handles millions
of players and how

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.060
Cloud Spanner is
essential to managing

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.230
high volumes of requests.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:25.310
My name is Yutty, customer
engineer from Google Cloud,

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.260
working with gaming
industry companies in Japan.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.570
Today, I'm with Taka, who
is an engineer from Colopl.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.810
The most difficult part
of launching a new game

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.100
is unpredictability.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.590
Once the game gets
popular, the server

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:47.150
often receives massive
requests in the short-term.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.980
Compute is very easy to scale.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.720
If you need more resources,
you simply add more servers.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.340
But the database
often [? begins ?]

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.240
a bottleneck because
legacy databases like MySQL

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.200
are not designed to
scale horizontally.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.750
So when the database
is overloaded,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.670
this is what happens.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.390
For game players, I think this
is the most frustrating error

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.130
message.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:17.950
If you are a loyal player,
maybe it's not a big deal.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.950
But if you are logging
in for the first time,

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.160
it may be your last time.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.860
Very few games can overcome
this type of error at launch.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.890
So how do game companies have
the database scaling program?

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:38.520
Well, in general, gaming
company need [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.920
And they use a technique
called sharding,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.450
which creates a single
database on different servers.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.660
For example,
sharding can be done

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.510
by players, by geolocations,
or by gaming platforms.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.050
This is a broad generalization.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:01.080
Sharding might be a solution
but can lead to hidden costs,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.620
as you can see in this iceberg.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.550
First, maintenance
costs-- for example,

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:11.440
if single database server
can handle 10,000 players,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.240
that means you need 100 servers
for every million players.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.130
I think no one wants to
manage hundreds of servers.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.260
Second, upgradability--
players from all over the world

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.440
will play at all
times of the day.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.400
Therefore, the database
has to function 24/7.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.820
It is impossible to find hour
of big maintenance window that

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.400
accommodates everyone.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.040
[? About ?] downtime,
traditional relational

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.500
databases offer
high availability

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.870
using a failover mechanism.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:55.040
This means if the database
server fails, it will recover.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.100
But it generates downtime.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.980
Today, Google Cloud
offers many types

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.640
of database, including
Firestore, Cloud SQL,

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.600
and third-party
partner solutions,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.640
which are designed for
various customer needs.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.500
Dragon Quest Walk has chosen
Cloud Spanner as their database

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:22.050
since it is the ideal, fully
managed, scalable database.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.540
Cloud Spanner combines the
benefit of relational database

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:29.465
structure with nonrelational
horizontal scale, which

00:03:29.465 --> 00:03:33.620
means Spanner is [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.750
Spanner also provides
horizontal scale in/scale out.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.490
It is fully managed and
has no planned downtime.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:46.630
In addition, Spanner can
provide up to [? 99.999% ?] SLA.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.120
As I mentioned earlier,
traditional database

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.360
have big hidden costs.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.390
By deploying your
game on Cloud Spanner,

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:01.310
you can [? draw ?] much
[INAUDIBLE] these hidden costs.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.000
Spanner can scale out with
one click and upgrades

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.410
automatically without downtime.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.110
Data is replicated
across zones, which

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.180
result in high availability.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.459
But how does this
translate into numbers?

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.420
When Enterprise Strategy Group,
which is a research firm,

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.270
did a three-year TCO analysis
for Spanner against us

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.960
other traditional
and cloud databases,

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.730
they found that you can
save 37% of the cost,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:42.570
compared to other cloud
solution, and 78% of cost,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.430
compared to other
on-premise solution.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.390
The savings mainly come from
hardware investment, as well as

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.450
many administrative
tasks that you

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.440
need to spend on to make
sure your database stays up

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.430
and running.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.140
Now, let's hear from
our customer, Colopl,

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:06.740
and how they adopted Cloud
Spanner in their game,

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.948
Dragon Quest Walk.

00:05:08.948 --> 00:05:09.740
TAKAYASU OYAMA: Hi.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.780
My name is Taka, and
I'm a software developer

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:15.490
for a Japanese game
company called Colopl.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.320
I will take over
from here and briefly

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.460
talk about how we use Cloud
Spanner in Dragon Quest Walk.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.530
Before I go into
the details, allow

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.810
me to do a brief
introduction about the game.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.780
The game is called
Dragon Quest Walk,

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.730
and it was developed by us
and published by Square Enix.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.860
The game is part of a
long-running Dragon Quest

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.010
series owned by Square Enix.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.660
So here are some
screenshots of what

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.050
the actual game looks like.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.100
The game is a merge
between location-based AR

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.570
and a traditional,
turn-based JRPG.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.940
The game is for mobile,
and it's available in Japan

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.340
for Android and iOS.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.280
So here are some numbers to give
you an insight of how much data

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.680
we process every day
with Cloud Spanner.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.490
So we released this game back
in September of last year.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.050
And I can't say
the exact numbers,

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.750
but we had a lot of users
register on the first day.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.810
What I can tell you is that we
had over 5 million downloads

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.640
on the first week.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.110
Currently, we
still have millions

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.690
of monthly active users,
and we process thousands

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.960
of queries per second
across hundreds of Spanner

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.850
nodes every day.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:26.350
Here's the agenda
for what I'm going

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.600
to be talking about today.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:32.430
First, I'll start by talking
about why we chose Spanner.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.520
Then, I will talk about
how we integrated Spanner

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.700
into our development.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.040
After that, I will talk
about some of the challenges

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.780
we faced with Spanner.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:43.610
So here are some
of the reasons why

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.880
we chose Spanner for this game,
starting with the first one

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.160
on the list, scaling in and out.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.010
So we used to use a self-managed
MySQL to handle our user data,

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.050
and we scaled it by splitting
data into horizontal shards.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:02.700
In our past titles, this
worked well for us at first.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.200
But when the numbers
of shards start

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.650
to grow into double digits, we
started to notice some problems

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:08.730
with this approach.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.630
The problem arose whenever
we wanted to scale.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:15.270
When we scaled these
databases in or out,

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.190
we had to scale
manually and we had

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:20.470
to scale once every few months.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:23.340
We would scale out to handle
the high traffic whenever

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.650
we released a new
feature or whenever

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.960
we did a seasonal
event, and then, we

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.860
would scale in to save costs
once the hype cooled down

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.630
and the traffic eased.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:36.060
Preparing and
executing these tasks

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:39.270
took hours and sometimes
days, depending on the scale,

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.240
and it was stressful
and time-consuming.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:46.810
By switching to Spanner, we were
able to do this with one click

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:50.520
and scaling would complete
within a few minutes.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.410
So that was reason number one.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.160
So the second reason
why we chose Spanner

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.210
was for stability.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.060
In our previous titles,
as I've mentioned earlier,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.120
we used a self-managed
MySQL, and we

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.370
used to get unexpected
crashes at least

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.740
once a month, mostly
due to host errors.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.370
With Spanner,
everything is managed

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:14.480
and all the problems are
handled in the background.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.180
Now, we've been running this
game for over six months now,

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.390
and we haven't had any major
downtimes, just minor ones,

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.210
and it wasn't Spanner's
fault. So I could confidently

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.850
say stability has been good.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.560
So that was reason number two.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:32.230
The third reason
we chose Spanner

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:35.960
was so we could write a more
readable and maintainable code.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:39.919
Previously, when we had to write
code for sharded databases,

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.230
our code had to be
infrastructure-aware,

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.100
which made it hard
to read and maintain.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.930
One example of this
would be something

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.330
like distributed
transactions, where

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.600
you have to make transactions
across different shards.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:54.460
This was hard to
understand, and it

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:57.860
was even harder to debug,
especially for the parts

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.350
where we had to roll back.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:03.380
Then, there are things like
replication delays, where

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.710
data retrieved from the replica
might not be up-to-date,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.980
so we had to be careful about
where we read our data from.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.140
After switching to
Spanner, we were

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.660
able to make inserts,
updates, and deletes

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.540
in a single transaction,
and replication delay

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:22.170
became a non-issue because
consistency is guaranteed.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:25.020
Now, I would like to talk
about how we integrated Spanner

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.020
into our development.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.300
When we first decided
to use Spanner,

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.880
we had the feeling it was
going to be a bumpy ride,

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.568
since no one at the company
had any experience with it

00:09:34.568 --> 00:09:36.110
and there was very
little information

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.870
available online at the time.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:40.930
So we did some
preparations to make sure

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:43.480
that the transition
from MySQL to Spanner

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.550
would be smooth and
painless as possible.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:48.110
So how did we do this?

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:52.120
Well, first, we created
a small, dedicated team.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.120
And by team, I mean
me and my colleague.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.910
So me and my colleague
perform some basic benchmarks

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:01.220
to check the performance.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:04.010
And once we confirm that
the performance was good,

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.050
we put in some extra
effort to making

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.420
a dedicated driver
for a PHP framework

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.220
we were using called Laravel.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.970
Doing so allowed us to use
pre-existing [? ORM, ?] which,

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.580
in turn, allowed us to
reuse existing libraries

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.710
we were using for
existing games.

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:24.430
By the way, we've uploaded
the driver we wrote on GitHub,

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:28.380
so please take a look at
it if you're interested.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:31.070
So once the driver was
written, we tested the driver

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:34.640
by taking an existing game and
replacing the MySQL driver that

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:37.660
was used with a Spanner driver.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.970
This worked well
because we get to see

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.288
how it performs
on real scenarios

00:10:42.288 --> 00:10:44.080
and allowed us to weed
out bugs we couldn't

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:47.140
catch with our automated tests.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.200
Doing this also helped us get
familiar with the database much

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.680
quicker than trying to apply
it to a game from scratch.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:58.520
After all that was done,
we integrated Spanner

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.850
into the main game
development cycle.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:04.370
By doing these
preparations, we were

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:06.980
able to fully integrate
Spanner into our game

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.430
in about two weeks.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.180
Now I'll talk about
some of the challenges

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.850
we faced with Spanner
and also talk about how

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:17.300
we overcame those challenges.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:20.860
First one on the
list is interleaving.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:23.803
Spanner has this concept
called interleaving,

00:11:23.803 --> 00:11:25.970
which is crucial for getting
optimal performance out

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:28.090
of your queries.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:29.590
Interleaving is a
concept of having

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:32.680
parent-child relationships
between tables so

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:36.490
that data for child tables
are inserted in the same node

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:39.080
as the parent table.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.890
This is essential because if
you have a has-many relation

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:45.950
and you try to get the related
rows from the children,

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:49.670
it's much more efficient to
look it up within the same node

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:53.980
rather than looking it up
across different nodes.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:57.150
So in order to account
for this new concept,

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:01.310
we scan through all the existing
schemas we wrote for MySQL

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.780
and added definitions
for interleaving

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:08.440
for tables that had a
parent-child relationship.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:11.310
At the same time, we
made sure all the indexes

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:13.650
that mimic the
relationship in MySQL

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.870
were removed, since they were
unnecessary when the tables are

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.350
interleaved.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.020
Another thing we made
sure was that all

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:25.260
queries that scanned the
interleave children included

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:28.730
the parent IDs when
doing a lookup.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:30.980
This was because omitting
it caused Spanner

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.610
to scan the entire database,
which degraded performance

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:36.400
tremendously.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.820
To be absolutely sure that
the parent ID was included

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:42.370
in all the queries,
we added some code

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:44.620
that listened to
all the query events

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.650
and threw a warning when
the parent ID was omitted.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.900
Next item on the list
is scale-in hiccups.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.700
So in this game, we scale
in and out fairly often,

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:59.190
based on how much traffic
we're expecting for the day.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.650
Scaling out is simple,
since all you have to do

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.140
is increase the number
of nodes in the UI

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:07.230
and wait a few minutes
for it to be deployed.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.670
Scaling in, however,
was a bit trickier.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.730
The actual task is the
same as scaling out,

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:17.160
but we noticed that when we
reduce the number of nodes

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:21.580
too aggressively, there was
a noticeable response delay.

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.130
These delays ranged
from a few milliseconds

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:27.320
up to maybe something
like 30 seconds.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:30.650
Sometimes, this also
caused the servers

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:34.810
to choke due to too many
connections being open.

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.290
So we resolved this
by forcing Spanner

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:41.820
to time out in three
seconds and return

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.280
a response to the client,
which asks the client

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:48.070
to retry the request
after a few seconds.

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:51.520
In addition to that, we now
try to decrease the nodes

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:54.640
in small increments,
since the range of delay

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:57.080
seems to be proportional
to the number of nodes.

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.560
we decrease.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:03.460
From what we can observe,
if we go easy and reduce

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.190
one node per five
minutes or so, we

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:09.700
manage to minimize the delay
enough that no retries occurred

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.840
on the client.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:16.380
The next item is handling
bursts of access to shared data.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.560
So the game has some
small sets of data, which

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:20.900
is shared across all users.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.410
These are things
like item properties

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.980
and monster map coordinates.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.780
We wanted to
replicate these data

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:31.680
so access to these data
could be distributed.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.340
The way to do it in
Spanner was to add a column

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:38.580
with a shard number and
insert copies of the data

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:40.840
with different shard numbers.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:43.040
Then, what you can do
is have the code select

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.130
one of the shards at random
and add that to the query

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:48.700
and that will
distribute the load.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.100
We thought about
doing it this way,

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.230
but we figured it was too
difficult to implement

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.180
into the framework at the time.

00:14:56.180 --> 00:15:00.010
So for shared data, we decided
to use a self-managed MySQL

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:04.240
instead, since it can replicate
and scale out easily if all you

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:07.420
have is a small set of data.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:11.820
The last item on the list is
handling launch day traffic.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:14.010
In order to talk
about this, I first

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:17.020
have to explain what a split is.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.150
Internally, Spanner
divides your data

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:22.630
into chunks called splits,
where a split can move around

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.250
between servers.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:26.560
And when there is
high load on a split,

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:29.320
it tries to slice it up
and add split boundaries

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:32.260
to distribute the load.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.620
This works well in
normal scenarios

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.050
where there is a gradual
increase in users

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.840
with gradual growth on load.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.490
But this was not
what we expected

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:43.950
to happen on launch day.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:45.780
What we expected to
happen on launch day

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.900
was thousands of
users registering

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.993
in a span of a few seconds.

00:15:51.993 --> 00:15:53.410
And we knew splitting
wasn't going

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:56.290
to be fast enough based
on numerous launch day

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:59.780
simulations we performed.

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:03.550
So in order to make this
work, we presplit the database

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:05.540
before the launch.

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:09.630
We called this process
warming up the database.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:13.150
The way we warmed it
up was rather simple.

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:15.190
We just forced the
splits to trigger

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:18.100
by slowly adding
thousands of dummy users

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:22.180
until it split enough to
handle the launch day traffic.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.810
After the split was complete,
we deleted the dummy users

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:31.660
since the split is retained even
after the users are deleted.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:36.790
So the method was simple, but
there was one small caveat.

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:41.890
These splits were only sustained
if there was access to them.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:44.500
Otherwise, these splits
start to merge again

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:47.330
after around 24 hours.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:50.960
So we made sure to
do the warm-up 24

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:54.060
hours before the actual launch.

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:57.600
The warm-up was a tedious
and time-consuming task,

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:01.200
but we're glad we did it because
the launch was a great success

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.280
with zero problems.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.619
Before I end, I just
wanted to show you

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:08.020
how our storage
architecture looks like

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:11.020
and briefly explain how
various services are used

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.430
in conjunction with Spanner.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:16.400
All the storage we use
is lined up on the right.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.829
Going from the top,
we have Spanner,

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:22.289
which holds all the
user data, as you know.

00:17:22.289 --> 00:17:25.520
Our analytical data,
like user action logs,

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:29.030
is streamed to BigQuery,
since BigQuery works better

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.530
for scanning large sums of data.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:35.430
Our master data, like monsters,
items, and location data,

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:38.580
is running on multiple
replicated MySQL servers,

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:42.160
where the load is distributed
using GKE service.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.080
Then, we have
Memorystore, which is

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.990
used for temporary caching
of frequently accessed data

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:50.730
to ease the load
on other storages.

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:53.310
And I completely
forgot to add to this,

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:57.790
but we also use Elasticsearch
to store parts of our user data

00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:59.310
so it can be used
to look up names

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.910
and other various attributes.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:04.480
That's all from me today.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:05.940
Thank you.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:07.340
YUTTY KAWAHARA: Thank you, Taka.

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:09.950
